Subject: gcc and single-step register VM builds compile again

What this does
  <emmintrin.h> is included once, near the top of quickjs.c, before the malloc/free poison defines; the three later includes are gone. regvm_fill_unwritten moves out of the QJS_REGVM_RECORD block, next to the code that uses it in both paths. The single-step handler's light-frame branch of apply-with-arguments is compiled only with QJS_REGVM_THREAD, which defines regvm_light_apply_arguments. QJS_AOT without QJS_REGVM_THREAD is now an #error. The single-step handler's REGVM_CMP_BRANCH #undefs the threaded handlers' macro of the same name before defining its own.

Why we need it
  Every gcc build (the default stack build included) stopped at gcc's emmintrin.h, which defines _mm_malloc/_mm_free through malloc and free after the poison. The register VM with QJS_REGVM_THREAD=0 (a documented CMake option, and what gcc gets) failed on regvm_fill_unwritten and regvm_light_apply_arguments. QJS_AOT with QJS_REGVM_THREAD=0 failed with 20 undeclared functions instead of saying it is unsupported. Every register VM build warned -Wmacro-redefined on REGVM_CMP_BRANCH.

Soundness
  Light frames exist only with QJS_REGVM_RECORD, which needs QJS_REGVM_THREAD, so without it the removed branch was unreachable. The include and the moved function change nothing in builds that compiled before. Compiled code is entered against the threaded chain's activation record, so QJS_AOT always needed QJS_REGVM_THREAD.

